<body>

<div class="hero">
  <div style="position:relative;z-index:1">
    <div class="hero-badge">Historical Trend Analysis</div>
    <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">A comprehensive data analysis of every Academy Award Best Original Score winner, 1934–2026 &mdash; spanning 93 ceremonies across nine decades of cinematic history.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<nav class="nav">
  <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
  <a href="#kpis">Key Metrics</a>
  <a href="#top-composers">Top Composers</a>
  <a href="#temporal">Temporal Patterns</a>
  <a href="#era-analysis">Era Analysis</a>
  <a href="#milestones">Milestones</a>
  <a href="#conclusions">Takeaways</a>
</nav>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 1: OVERVIEW -->
<section id="overview">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 01</div>
    <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    <p class="lead">From the sixth Academy Awards ceremony in 1934 to the 98th in 2026, the Best Original Score Oscar has been awarded across 93 ceremonies — a record spanning the birth of sound cinema through the streaming era.</p>

    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score is one of Hollywood's oldest competitive categories, introduced just six years after The Jazz Singer ushered in the era of synchronized sound. Initially called "Best Score," it has undergone several name changes and structural shifts — including periods where the Academy split the category between dramatic and comedy/musical scores — but this dataset consolidates one canonical winner per year, producing an unbroken 93-entry record.</p>

    <p>The category's winners read as a mirror of Hollywood's creative evolution: from the lavish orchestral tapestries of the studio system's Golden Age, through the countercultural experiments of the 1960s–70s, the blockbuster era of the 1980s–90s, and into the globally diverse contemporary landscape. Over nine decades, the award has been claimed by 69 distinct composer entries — some legends with four wins, others one-time honorees who captured a singular moment perfectly.</p>

    <p>This report examines every winner through three analytical lenses: <strong>who</strong> dominated the landscape, <strong>when</strong> patterns of dominance emerged and dissolved, and <strong>how</strong> each era's defining composers reflect the broader cultural and cinematic context of their time.</p>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <div class="chart-title">All 93 Oscar Best Original Score Winners — Timeline by Year</div>
      <canvas id="timelineChart" height="120"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ KPI SECTION -->
<section id="kpis" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#1F1812 0%,#1A1410 100%)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Key Performance Indicators</div>
    <h2>By the Numbers</h2>
    <p class="lead">Four headline statistics that frame the competitive landscape of Oscar scoring history.</p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">69</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Total Unique Composers</div>
        <div class="kpi-note">Distinct winning entries across 93 ceremonies</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">4</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Maximum Wins — Single Composer</div>
        <div class="kpi-note">Shared by John Barry, John Williams &amp; Ludwig Göransson</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">25.8%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Repeat Winner Rate</div>
        <div class="kpi-note">24 of 93 ceremonies won by a previous winner</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">1980s</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Decade — Most Unique Winners</div>
        <div class="kpi-note">10 distinct composers won across 10 ceremonies</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-muted)">The repeat winner rate of 25.8% means that roughly one in four ceremonies has rewarded a proven master over fresh talent — a notable but not dominant pattern, suggesting the Oscar electorate generally rewards novelty and singular achievement over established pedigrees.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 2: TOP COMPOSERS -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 02</div>
    <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>
    <p class="lead">Fourteen composers have won the Best Original Score Oscar more than once. Three stand alone at the top with four wins each — a record that has now been matched by a composer still active today.</p>

    <p>The win-count leaderboard is dominated by a remarkable tie at the summit: <strong>John Barry</strong>, <strong>John Williams</strong>, and <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> each hold four wins. What makes this three-way tie historically striking is the generational spread — Barry's wins span 1966–1990, Williams's span 1975–1993, and Göransson's 2018–2026. The latter's four wins arrived in just nine years, making his trajectory historically unprecedented in terms of pace.</p>

    <div class="composer-bar-wrap">
      <!-- Rank 1–3: 4 wins -->
      <div class="composer-row">
        <span class="composer-rank">#1</span>
        <span class="composer-name">John Barry</span>
        <div class="composer-bar-bg"><div class="composer-bar-fill" style="width:100%"></div></div>
        <span class="composer-wins">4</span>
        <span class="composer-years">1966 · 1968 · 1985 · 1990</span>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-row">
        <span class="composer-rank">#1</span>
        <span class="composer-name">John Williams</span>
        <div class="composer-bar-bg"><div class="composer-bar-fill" style="width:100%"></div></div>
        <span class="composer-wins">4</span>
        <span class="composer-years">1975 · 1977 · 1982 · 1993</span>
      </div>
