<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS -->
<section id="era-analysis" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#1F1812 0%,#1A1410 100%)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 04</div>
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p class="lead">Grouping Oscar history into four broad eras reveals how the identity of the "winning score" has evolved — from European-trained studio craftsmen to genre-bending contemporary auteurs.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">

      <div class="era-card">
        <div class="era-title">Golden Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1934–1960 · 27 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat-row">
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">27</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Ceremonies</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">20</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Unique Winners</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">3</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Max Wins</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="era-desc">The studio system era produced film music's foundational architects. <strong>Max Steiner</strong> and <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong> each won three times; <strong>Miklos Rozsa</strong> and <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> claimed two each. These European-trained composers — many refugees from Nazi Germany — fused post-Romantic orchestral language with Hollywood's narrative demands, creating the template of "classical" film scoring. Collaborative multi-composer wins (Stagecoach 1939, Pinocchio 1941) reflected the assembly-line nature of studio production.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card">
        <div class="era-title">Silver Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1961–1980 · 20 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat-row">
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">20</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Ceremonies</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">17</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Unique Winners</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">2</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Max Wins</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="era-desc">The Silver Age brought stylistic pluralism. British composers rose — <strong>John Barry</strong> and <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> each won twice, with Barry's Bond-adjacent sound and Jarre's sweeping epics (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>, <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>) defining a new cinematic romanticism. The era also saw jazz (<em>Breakfast at Tiffany's</em>), folk-pop (<em>Butch Cassidy</em>), and proto-electronic sounds (<em>Love Story</em>) claim the prize, reflecting Hollywood's openness to contemporary popular idioms. The 1970s introduced <strong>John Williams</strong>, whose Jaws (1975) and Star Wars (1977) signaled a neoclassical turn that would define the next era.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card">
        <div class="era-title">Modern Era</div>
        <div class="era-years">1981–2000 · 20 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat-row">
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">20</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Ceremonies</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">16</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Unique Winners</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">3</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Max Wins</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="era-desc">The Modern Era was defined by two dominant forces: the remarkable diversity of the 1980s (ten unique winners in ten years) and <strong>Alan Menken</strong>'s Disney dominance in the early 1990s (three wins in four years: <em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>). The era was also notable for experimental outliers — Vangelis's synthesizer score for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981), Herbie Hancock's jazz fusion (<em>Round Midnight</em>, 1986), and the Last Emperor's three-composer collective (Sakamoto, Byrne, Cong Su). John Williams added two more wins (1982, 1993) and Hans Zimmer opened his account in 1994.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card">
        <div class="era-title">Contemporary Era</div>
        <div class="era-years">2001–2026 · 26 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat-row">
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">26</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Ceremonies</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">20</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Unique Winners</div></div>
          <div class="era-stat"><div class="era-stat-val">4</div><div class="era-stat-lab">Max Wins</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="era-desc">The Contemporary Era is the most globally diverse, with winners from Argentina (<strong>Gustavo Santaolalla</strong>), India (A.R. Rahman), Iceland (Hildur Guðnadóttir), Germany (Volker Bertelmann), and Sweden (<strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong>). Howard Shore's consecutive LOTR wins (2001, 2003) mirrored prior franchise dominance. The era ends with Göransson's historic four wins — and three between 2023–2026 — signaling a new dominant voice in contemporary film scoring.</div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <div class="chart-title">Composer Wins by Era — Concentration vs. Diversity</div>
      <canvas id="eraChart" height="90"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 5: MILESTONES -->
<section id="milestones">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 05</div>
    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
    <p class="lead">Across 93 ceremonies, a handful of moments stand out as true historical inflection points — firsts, lasts, streaks, and records that frame the category's evolution.</p>

    <ul class="milestone-list">

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1934</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>The First Winner.</strong> <strong>Louis Silvers</strong> won for <em>One Night of Love</em> at the 6th Academy Awards — the inaugural year of the Best Score category. Silvers's win for a romantic opera film set an early precedent for the Academy's affinity for sweeping, genre-anchored scores.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1939</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First Collaborative Win.</strong> The <em>Stagecoach</em> score shared between <strong>Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken</strong> remains the largest group to ever share the award — four composers credited for a single score, reflecting how studio orchestration often involved multiple hands.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1950–51</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First Back-to-Back Wins.</strong> <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> became the first composer to win in consecutive years, claiming the award for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> (1950) and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> (1951) — a feat that would not be replicated until Gustavo Santaolalla's 2005–2006 run.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1981</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First Synthesizer Win.</strong> <strong>Vangelis</strong>'s all-electronic score for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> was the first Oscar win for a score composed entirely on synthesizers — a landmark that signaled the Academy's willingness to recognize non-orchestral film music.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1994</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Hans Zimmer Opens His Account.</strong> <strong>Hans Zimmer</strong>'s win for <em>The Lion King</em> would be followed by a 27-year wait before his second win — for <em>Dune</em> (2021). That 27-year gap is the longest in this dataset between a composer's first and subsequent Oscar win.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2005–06</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Santaolalla's Back-to-Back.</strong> <strong>Gustavo Santaolalla</strong> became only the second composer (after Waxman) to win back-to-back, with <em>Brokeback Mountain</em> (2005) and <em>Babel</em> (2006) — the first consecutive wins in 55 years.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2015</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Morricone's Long-Awaited Win.</strong> <strong>Ennio Morricone</strong> — widely regarded as one of the greatest film composers of all time — did not win a competitive Oscar until age 87, when he took the prize for <em>The Hateful Eight</em>. (He received an Honorary Oscar in 2006.) His win stands as one of the most celebrated in the category's history.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2019</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First Female Solo Winner.</strong> <strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir</strong>'s win for <em>Joker</em> (2019) made her the first woman to win the Best Original Score Oscar as a sole composer — a landmark moment in a category that has historically been dominated by men.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2023–24</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Göransson's Back-to-Back.</strong> <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> won in 2023 and 2024, both times for <em>Oppenheimer</em> — matching Santaolalla's consecutive feat and becoming the most recent composer to win back-to-back.</span>
      </li>

      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2026</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Most Recent Winner — Record Pace.</strong> <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong>'s fourth win for <em>Sinners</em> (2026) brought his career total to four in nine years — the fastest accumulation of four Oscar wins in the category's 92-year history. At the time of his fourth win, he is the active composer with the most wins in the category.</span>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<section id="conclusions" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#1F1812 0%,#1A1410 100%)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    <p class="lead">Five evidence-based conclusions from 93 years of Oscar scoring history — and what they suggest about the category's trajectory.</p>

    <div class="takeaway-grid">

      <div class="takeaway">
        <div class="takeaway-num">01</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Dominance Is Rare but Decisive</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">Only 14 of 69 distinct winning entries (20%) represent multi-win composers, yet they account for 38 of 93 ceremonies (41%). When a composer breaks through, they tend to break through repeatedly. The record of four wins has been set three times — and Göransson's pace suggests it may be surpassed in the coming decade.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <div class="takeaway-num">02</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Decades of Diversity Precede Concentrations of Power</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">The 1980s' perfect 10-of-10 diversity score was followed by Alan Menken's three wins in four early-1990s years. The 2010s' near-perfect diversity (9 of 10) preceded Göransson's four wins in the 2020s. This oscillation between openness and consolidation appears structural.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <div class="takeaway-num">03</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Global Diversification Is the Defining Trend of the Contemporary Era</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">The Golden Age was almost entirely American studio composers (many European-born); the Silver Age was dominated by British and French names; the Contemporary era has produced winners from Argentina, India, Iceland, Japan, Sweden, Germany, and the UK. The Oscar electorate has become genuinely global in its recognition.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
